body:
This adds Kitty graphics to the terminal snapshot format. A restored terminal now shows the same images in the same places, including scrollback, animations, virtual placements, and a chunked transmission that was still in progress when the snapshot was taken.

The records are optional. A decoder without Kitty graphics support or by choice can skip them. 

Images and placements are restored before READY, so the terminal handed to the caller at READY already has its full layout. But the image _data_ (pixel data) is sent _after READY_. This is done to optimize a time-to-render: a renderer can render the terminal and all cells in the proper layout by READY, but lacks the image data still.

Image pixels arrive after READY as their own records, one per image or animation frame. The encoder orders payloads by need: images visible in the shown viewport first, then each scrollback image right after the history page that first shows it, then everything else. 

Pixels are stored raw. Snapshots are normally compressed in transit, and on real images that compressor does at least as well on raw pixels as on pre-compressed ones, so compressing here would only add encoder time. Pointless.

## C Example

The incremental decoder reports each image's pixels as a progress event alongside history pages, and the C API exposes this, plus image counts and pending bytes per screen for progress UI:

```c
uint64_t pending = 0;
ghostty_snapshot_decoder_get(
    decoder, GHOSTTY_SNAPSHOT_DECODER_DATA_KITTY_PENDING_BYTES_PRIMARY,
    &pending);

while (ghostty_snapshot_decoder_next(decoder) == GHOSTTY_SUCCESS) {
  GhosttySnapshotDecoderProgressKind kind;
  ghostty_snapshot_decoder_get(
      decoder, GHOSTTY_SNAPSHOT_DECODER_DATA_PROGRESS_KIND, &kind);
  if (kind == GHOSTTY_SNAPSHOT_DECODER_PROGRESS_KIND_IMAGE) {
    size_t bytes = 0;
    ghostty_snapshot_decoder_get(
        decoder, GHOSTTY_SNAPSHOT_DECODER_DATA_PROGRESS_IMAGE_BYTES, &bytes);
    // Advance a progress bar, redraw the image, etc.
  }
}
```

**AI usage:** I designed the binary layout and big decisions, I had Fable+Opus do most of the implementation plus review (in addition to my human review). I wrote the docs myself after it had done that. The encoding implementation (kitty.zig) is _fine_, there's a bunch of places I think I'd reorder or do things slightly differently but at this stage I was far more interested in getting the binary layout, fixtures, etc. right over the raw how it works.
